            <ArticleTitle>The index case for the fungal meningitis outbreak in the United States.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2119-25</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1056/NEJMoa1212292</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Persistent neutrophilic meningitis presents a diagnostic challenge, because the differential diagnosis is broad and includes atypical infectious causes. We describe a case of persistent neutrophilic meningitis due to Aspergillus fumigatus in an immunocompetent man who had no evidence of sinopulmonary or cutaneous disease. An epidural glucocorticoid injection was identified as a potential route of entry for this organism into the central nervous system, and the case was reported to the state health department.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
